<section xml:id="notes-week-15">
    <title>Week 15</title>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-chap8">
        <title>Spacetime Physics Chapter 8</title>

        <p>
            <alert>Reading Quiz 19 covers 8.1 through 8.5</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading Quiz 20 covers 8.6 through 8.8</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Chapter 8 is the relativity version of chapter 4
                        of K&amp;K: we're choosing systems, drawing
                        before and after pictures, and using conservation
                        of 4-momentum. Our examples here will be photons,
                        atomic nuclei, and other high energy particles
                        instead of rockets. It's at that scale where
                        using the relativistic notion of 4-momentum is
                        crucial for the proper description of collisions.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Conceptually, sections 8.3 and 8.4 are the most important
                        ones. Section 8.3 and the Dialog at the end of the chapter
                        have very thorough discussions on the concepts of system
                        mass, and what we mean when we say things like 
                        <q>mass can be converted into energy</q>, which is a
                        very subtle statement.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Section 8.4 then shows us that special relativity has
                        no issue with the concept of a massless particle, but
                        it does require some care to describe the 4-momentum
                        of something like a photon. Sections 8.5 and 8.6
                        show you how to apply conservation of 4-momentum
                        to some exciting atomic processes.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Sections 8.7 and 8.8 are about nuclear physics. This is
                        <em>the</em> application of <m>E=mc^2</m>, and one of the
                        lessons here is that we can learn a good amount nuclei
                        by measuring their masses. In particular, the 
                        <q>mass per nucleon</q> of a nucleus is an important
                        quantity that helps characterize its stability.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        One thing to note is that explaining <em>why</em> the
                        masses of nuclei are what they are is not something
                        the textbook is interested in, as that is firmly in
                        the realm of quantum mechanics/field theory, and 
                        modeling nuclei is still an active area of research.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </subsection>
 
</section>